= Comparative wiki research register =

'''Status: Active research register.''' Reviewed 2026-08-20. This page tracks external knowledge-system research relevant to BITwiki architecture, navigation, governance, coverage, community, and security. A reviewed source is evidence, not automatic design authority.

== Why this exists ==

Wikipedia is not one system. It is simultaneously an encyclopedia, category graph, link graph, portal system, set of editorial processes, governance community, maintenance machine, multilingual federation, software ecosystem, and object of academic study.

The wider wiki/encyclopedia ecosystem is even more varied. BITwiki should learn comparatively rather than cargo-cult one English Wikipedia convention.

The research program is therefore intentionally fractal:

<pre>
Wikipedia
  → English Wikipedia
  → 348 active language editions
  → sister Wikimedia projects
  → Wikimedia governance / Meta-Wiki
  → wiki communities outside Wikimedia
  → online encyclopedias outside wiki culture
  → specialist knowledge systems
  → academic studies of all of the above
</pre>

== Research dimensions ==

Each knowledge system or wiki edition can eventually be compared across a common schema:

* mission and scope;
* audience;
* editorial model;
* governance model;
* contributor identity and permissions;
* review / approval / deletion workflow;
* namespaces and content/admin separation;
* categories and taxonomy;
* portals / contents / outlines / lists / indices;
* semantic structure and metadata;
* quality assessment;
* automation / bots / AI;
* revision and provenance model;
* source/reliability policy;
* contentious-topic handling;
* abuse, censorship, legal, and contributor-safety exposure;
* language/localization model;
* scale, activity, and maintenance burden;
* software / extensions;
* licensing and reuse;
* distinctive strengths;
* known failure modes.

This should become structured comparative data rather than an indefinitely growing prose notebook.

